A box is four layers, inside out: **content**, **padding**, **border**, **margin**.

```css title="card.css"
.card {
  box-sizing: border-box; /* width includes padding and border */
  width: 300px;
  padding: 24px;
  border: 2px solid;
}
```

With `border-box`, the card is exactly 300px wide. Without it, it is 352px — and that is where many overflowing layouts come from.

Flexbox lays children out along **one** line — a row or a column.

```css title="toolbar.css"
.toolbar { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px; }
.toolbar .spacer { flex: 1; }
```

The spacer grows to fill the leftover room, pushing everything after it to the far end.

`flex: 1` is shorthand for three numbers: grow `1`, shrink `1`, basis `0`.

- **basis** is the starting size;
- **grow** shares out the extra room;
- **shrink** shares out the missing room.

